1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,560
Yeah. Hi everyone, I'm Pete. I'm Daniel. And we're software engineers at Portswiger,

2
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:12,600
working on our cross product AR offering called Burp AI. We're talking about the story of what's

3
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:18,760
gone into its evolution and some of the techniques you can use to build agentic systems and improve

4
00:00:18,760 --> 00:00:24,720
them for web application security. So what we're going to show you today then? Well, first we're

5
00:00:24,720 --> 00:00:30,960
going to cover why we think AI in AppSec and Burp is a reasonable idea. Then we're going to go over

6
00:00:30,960 --> 00:00:35,860
how the Burp AI launch went, what we learned from it, what we learned from the initial features.

7
00:00:37,760 --> 00:00:42,380
Next, the approach and techniques to consider when building and improving an agentic system

8
00:00:42,380 --> 00:00:48,260
and the importance of tools and how the model context protocol or MCP can help.

9
00:00:49,040 --> 00:00:54,300
And then finally, how you can use and experiment with AI to improve your own workflows.

10
00:00:54,720 --> 00:00:58,320
And we sprinkled in a couple of short demos throughout the presentation as well.

11
00:01:00,060 --> 00:01:06,520
So it's pretty clear that right now in 2025, everyone from Fortune 500 companies to high school

12
00:01:06,520 --> 00:01:11,420
students working from their bedrooms are building products of AI. And these range from simple

13
00:01:11,420 --> 00:01:16,060
chatbots to fully automated systems for creating websites without touching any code.

14
00:01:17,420 --> 00:01:24,580
So what makes AppSec interesting for AI? Well, AI has proved to be extremely

15
00:01:24,580 --> 00:01:29,300
disruptive in the software development industry, with workflows evolving to incorporate these tools

16
00:01:29,300 --> 00:01:34,260
and benefit from the productivity gains. And this is translating over into the cybersecurity domain.

17
00:01:35,060 --> 00:01:41,140
So the ISC2 pulse survey showed that 30% of security teams have already integrated AI tools,

18
00:01:41,140 --> 00:01:48,580
with another 42% actively evaluating or testing them. And crucially, 70% of adopters report increased

19
00:01:48,580 --> 00:01:55,460
effectiveness. And AppSec is a domain in which there is like a wealth of opportunities beyond chatbots.

20
00:01:57,940 --> 00:02:03,140
Once we recognised the potential, we went about launching Burp AI with a set of five features.

21
00:02:03,140 --> 00:02:08,580
And the idea was to cast the net wide addressing different pain points where AI could provide immediate value,

22
00:02:09,140 --> 00:02:12,100
solving problems that are much more difficult to address without AI.

23
00:02:12,100 --> 00:02:18,020
And then after the release, when we looked at usage trends and gathered feedback, one feature stood out.

24
00:02:18,020 --> 00:02:21,540
That was Explorer Issue. It was quickly becoming the most popular.

25
00:02:22,340 --> 00:02:27,540
And this confirmed our assumptions about where AI can make the biggest impact in web app security testing.

26
00:02:28,820 --> 00:02:34,100
So to give a little bit of background on the Explorer Issue feature, it lets you take a scanner found issue

27
00:02:34,100 --> 00:02:42,980
and dig deeper, uncovering more context and sometimes escalating the finding using tools such as Repeater and Intruder to interact further with the site.

28
00:02:44,580 --> 00:02:47,460
And in practice, as the tool matured, we saw something interesting.

29
00:02:48,020 --> 00:02:52,180
Many users were creating their own custom issues and running Explorer Issue on those.

30
00:02:53,220 --> 00:02:55,300
And this gave us two key insights.

31
00:02:55,300 --> 00:02:59,540
One, users wanted more control over the initial information being sent to the AI agent.

32
00:02:59,540 --> 00:03:05,060
And two, they wanted to use the agent outside of scanner issues to get insights from other contexts.

33
00:03:06,820 --> 00:03:11,700
So, ultimately, the power of features like Explorer Issue come down to its agentic approach.

34
00:03:12,340 --> 00:03:19,220
An agent isn't just a one-shot prompt. It can perform an action, learn from the outcome, and adapt its direction of attack for the next step.

35
00:03:20,020 --> 00:03:25,060
That iterative loop allows the AI to tackle harder problems more effectively and autonomously.

36
00:03:25,060 --> 00:03:29,300
The agent can refine its strategy of each attempt just like a human tester would.

37
00:03:30,660 --> 00:03:31,940
So that all sounds great.

38
00:03:31,940 --> 00:03:37,380
Agentic AI systems seem really powerful for automating tedious or potentially complicated tasks.

39
00:03:37,380 --> 00:03:40,820
But a natural follow-up question is, how do I make them better in my domain?

40
00:03:42,260 --> 00:03:48,820
So the first instinct for many is to take the AI model at the center of your agentic system and improve and specialize it for your domain.

41
00:03:49,380 --> 00:03:54,100
For those of you familiar with LLMs and Transformers, you may have heard of fine-tuning.

42
00:03:54,100 --> 00:03:58,900
It's the process of updating the model weights with a task-specific training set.

43
00:04:00,580 --> 00:04:04,500
In the best case, this can lead to a specialized model with the behavior you need.

44
00:04:04,500 --> 00:04:10,740
For example, you might be able to get the model to adopt the right tone of voice, or handle domain-specific jargon,

45
00:04:11,540 --> 00:04:14,900
or consistently perform a repetitive task with high accuracy.

46
00:04:14,900 --> 00:04:22,420
In highly stable problem spaces, this can result in a model that outperforms a general-purpose model on that particular task.

47
00:04:23,940 --> 00:04:27,780
However, in practice, fine-tuning has some significant drawbacks.

48
00:04:27,780 --> 00:04:31,780
It can be difficult and labor-intensive to gather and prepare the necessary data.

49
00:04:31,780 --> 00:04:37,060
Updating the weights on a cutting-edge LLM uses a tremendous amount of computational power,

50
00:04:37,620 --> 00:04:44,420
and even after all of that, you're likely to end up with a reduction in the capability for general skills, such as tool-calling,

51
00:04:44,420 --> 00:04:49,300
and you will have to repeat the process all over again if you migrate to a new foundation model.

52
00:04:50,260 --> 00:04:52,900
Fine-tuning can also quickly become out of date,

53
00:04:52,900 --> 00:04:57,780
and especially when the domain is changing as rapidly as it does in web app security testing.

54
00:05:00,100 --> 00:05:03,380
This is an opening sentence from Rich Sutton's The Bitter Lesson,

55
00:05:03,380 --> 00:05:05,940
and it still rings true when building agentic systems.

56
00:05:06,980 --> 00:05:11,620
The biggest lesson that can be read from 70 years of AI research is that general methods

57
00:05:11,620 --> 00:05:15,940
that leverage computation are ultimately the most effective and by a large margin.

58
00:05:15,940 --> 00:05:23,380
So, when building agentic systems for a fast-moving domain such as web app pen testing,

59
00:05:23,380 --> 00:05:24,980
this lesson is especially relevant.

60
00:05:25,540 --> 00:05:30,420
Models change rapidly and new foundation models arrive with leaps in general capability.

61
00:05:31,060 --> 00:05:37,060
If we invest too heavily in over-optimizing architectures, complex workflows, or custom fine-tunes

62
00:05:37,780 --> 00:05:42,100
that don't scale with compute and foundation model improvements,

63
00:05:42,100 --> 00:05:46,500
then we risk building brittle systems that can become obsolete in under a year.

64
00:05:47,860 --> 00:05:51,140
So, the key takeaway is the sanity check where effort is being spent.

65
00:05:51,700 --> 00:05:54,580
If a new workflow or structure only works for today's model,

66
00:05:54,580 --> 00:05:58,660
or if it requires constant re-engineering, then it might not be worth the investment.

67
00:05:59,780 --> 00:06:05,300
Cool, thanks Pete. So, I guess while taking all that into account, is there something else that we

68
00:06:05,300 --> 00:06:09,220
can do when building these agentic pen testing systems?

69
00:06:09,220 --> 00:06:16,100
So, yeah, instead of focusing on improving the LLM model at the core of the agent,

70
00:06:16,100 --> 00:06:21,460
we can shift to something else that's much more flexible, the input that we actually feed that agent.

71
00:06:21,460 --> 00:06:26,820
This encompasses everything from the user's prompts to the tools that we provide the LLM,

72
00:06:26,820 --> 00:06:31,700
creating a dynamic system that provides the right context in the right format at the right time.

73
00:06:32,740 --> 00:06:38,500
This usually gives general purpose models everything they need to accomplish a wide variety of complex tasks.

74
00:06:39,220 --> 00:06:47,140
The term recently coined for this is context engineering, and all of this information is used to produce the next output or tool call in the agentic loop.

75
00:06:47,140 --> 00:06:56,420
So, one of the simplest and most powerful ways that you can actually give your agent high quality context is just through human interaction.

76
00:06:56,420 --> 00:07:03,540
This often takes the form of a prompt, which you may be familiar with from popular tools such as ChatGPT.

77
00:07:04,660 --> 00:07:11,780
Another way of gathering this human interaction can be achieved by allowing the agent to ask for it when it needs it as a tool call.

78
00:07:11,780 --> 00:07:16,420
However, we need to be careful about when and how often we do this.

79
00:07:17,060 --> 00:07:22,580
Every time the agent turns to a human for help, it introduces friction into the workflow.

80
00:07:22,580 --> 00:07:31,620
And if it happens too frequently, it stops feeling like this magical intelligent assistant and starts feeling like you're just manually performing the task yourself in a more clumsy way.

81
00:07:31,620 --> 00:07:38,260
The principle is to maximize autonomy while reserving human input for high leverage interventions.

82
00:07:40,420 --> 00:07:51,380
So, following on from these lessons that we learned as a team from the explore issue feature, we really wanted this agentic capability to be available to a wider audience and with more user control over the context.

83
00:07:51,380 --> 00:07:58,740
Burp repeater is that next step and it's a tool that enables you to, sorry, Burp AI and repeater is that next step.

84
00:07:58,740 --> 00:08:06,980
And repeater itself is a tool that enables you to modify and send an interesting HTTP or WebSocket message over and over.

85
00:08:07,780 --> 00:08:11,140
It's perhaps the most popular and fundamental tool in Burp Suite.

86
00:08:11,140 --> 00:08:23,540
Giving Burp users the ability to easily access an agentic AI system within the context of repeater really improves the flexibility of it and puts in a place which integrates smoothly with a wider range of workflows.

87
00:08:25,300 --> 00:08:29,140
So, if you haven't used the Web Security Academy before, one, I recommend it.

88
00:08:29,140 --> 00:08:36,740
And two, it's a free online collection of labs where you can learn and test your skills on a wide variety of realistic vulnerabilities.

89
00:08:36,740 --> 00:08:48,340
So, I'm about to show you a quick demo of Burp AI and repeater completing a reflected XSS lab from the Web Academy to demonstrate sort of like the power of these agentic systems and how they work in practice.

90
00:08:49,540 --> 00:08:52,980
So, let's see if this video will actually load for you guys.

91
00:08:53,460 --> 00:08:53,740
Cool.

92
00:08:54,180 --> 00:08:55,500
So, we're here in repeater.

93
00:08:55,940 --> 00:09:05,060
You can see that I've already sent a request to the lab homepage and we have this new Burp AI button up in the top with a pre-written prompt here.

94
00:09:05,060 --> 00:09:09,980
It does save your prompt if you close that pop-up so you don't have to leave it open.

95
00:09:10,380 --> 00:09:15,620
And we include context from both the request and response when we're sending it to the LLM.

96
00:09:16,060 --> 00:09:21,500
So, if you click over on that post, you're able to jump right over to the dashboard and see the running task.

97
00:09:23,340 --> 00:09:26,860
And here's one that's run before with the exact same prompt.

98
00:09:26,860 --> 00:09:34,300
So, it's going to be looking in the search parameter for reflection and it's going to try and exploit this and solve the lab.

99
00:09:34,300 --> 00:09:45,300
So, it's going to go through that agentic loop that we talked about before where it's going to try something, observe what it's done, and reason about it so it can make its next action.

100
00:09:45,840 --> 00:09:53,440
And here you can sort of see it working through how you would do this XSS lab, figuring out how it can escape the JavaScript string.

101
