Cool.

106
00:10:23,460 --> 00:10:25,660
So, that's Burp AI and Repeater.

107
00:10:27,280 --> 00:10:29,460
Let me hop on back over here.

108
00:10:30,480 --> 00:10:37,460
So, following on from this, tools are really the next critical part of context engineering for an agentic system.

109
00:10:37,460 --> 00:10:44,840
The selection and capabilities of these tools that you provide can really make or break the effectiveness of that system.

110
00:10:45,480 --> 00:10:48,740
They're how an agent interacts with the world and gathers information.

111
00:10:49,620 --> 00:10:59,840
When developing tools for AI agents, it's best to understand how they affect your context and add safety guardrails, as well as adding controls for when tools are available in that context.

112
00:10:59,840 --> 00:11:13,640
A significant challenge in the case of a tool like Repeater is how do you handle the response data returned from an HTTP request, which may be massive, while keeping the valuable information for the agent to use to work towards its goals.

113
00:11:15,500 --> 00:11:22,680
So, when it comes to APIs for AI tools, Model Context Protocol, or MCP, immediately comes to mind.

114
00:11:22,680 --> 00:11:41,140
Hopefully, some of you have heard of it, but if you haven't, it's a widely adopted API in the industry, and it's growing a large number of useful capabilities, and it kind of makes it the obvious choice for exposing tools to agents at the moment.

115
00:11:41,960 --> 00:11:45,260
It's often analogized as the USB-C for AI.

116
00:11:45,740 --> 00:11:51,340
It's a universal connector that makes it easy to connect your different AI platforms to external tools and data.

117
00:11:51,340 --> 00:11:55,920
In the protocol, the MCP server provides the context.

118
00:11:56,200 --> 00:12:01,760
That could be access to your burp tools, such as Repeater or Intruder, or something like the files on your computer.

119
00:12:03,140 --> 00:12:09,060
And then the MCP client on the other end then pulls that context in so the AI can actually use it.

120
00:12:10,160 --> 00:12:16,400
So, MCP has some really desirable capabilities built in, such as tool discovery and interoperability.

121
00:12:16,400 --> 00:12:23,400
So, this means that agents can query which tools are available and what they can do without requiring hard-coded integrations.

122
00:12:24,460 --> 00:12:26,640
It also supports continuous streams of data.

123
00:12:27,020 --> 00:12:36,260
So, for security testing, this could enable long-running tasks, such as crawling or brute-forcing, where partial results can be consumed immediately by the agent.

124
00:12:36,260 --> 00:12:39,460
And finally, it has fine-grained controls.

125
00:12:40,060 --> 00:12:43,940
MCP lets tool providers declare what tools and actions are available.

126
00:12:44,500 --> 00:12:52,380
For offensive tooling like burp, this creates an extra layer of assurance and control when exposing potentially destructive operations to those AI agents.

127
00:12:52,380 --> 00:13:04,920
So, if you guys want to try out MCP with burp suite using your own AI client, I've released an open-source MCP server extension in the in-product extension market called the BAP Store.

128
00:13:05,340 --> 00:13:11,740
It's available in both the burp suite community and pro versions, so it's not limited by a paywall or anything.

129
00:13:11,740 --> 00:13:17,280
And it's still being improved, and we're happy to take new issues and PRs submitted on the GitHub repo.

130
00:13:18,680 --> 00:13:26,600
So, here's a real quick demo of the MCP server extension being used in burp to perform a pretty simple operation of just changing the color theme.

131
